00:03:10.270 --> 00:03:13.520
So I just want to
go in a nutshell

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:15.820
what Dunnhumby kind
of do and how we work.

00:03:15.820 --> 00:03:18.700
So clients will send
their data to us.

00:03:18.700 --> 00:03:21.790
And when they do that, we will
assign a team of data engineers

00:03:21.790 --> 00:03:23.270
to that client.

00:03:23.270 --> 00:03:25.930
And it's their responsibility
to take the incoming data,

00:03:25.930 --> 00:03:29.800
transform it into what we call a
common data model that can then

00:03:29.800 --> 00:03:33.220
be used by our products,
and by our services,

00:03:33.220 --> 00:03:35.510
by our data scientists
throughout the company.

00:03:35.510 --> 00:03:37.330
So that's the role of
our data engineers.

00:03:37.330 --> 00:03:40.440
We have products which consume
that common data model.

00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:42.010
And we have data
scientists which

00:03:42.010 --> 00:03:45.190
provide consultancy
services running algorithms

00:03:45.190 --> 00:03:46.680
over that common data model.

00:03:46.680 --> 00:03:49.060
And finally, we
deliver services back

00:03:49.060 --> 00:03:53.952
to our clients in the way of
APIs and data visualizations.

00:03:56.850 --> 00:04:00.422
So I said we've been in
this business for 25 years,

00:04:00.422 --> 00:04:02.630
but our business has been
changing over the past five

00:04:02.630 --> 00:04:03.140
years.

00:04:03.140 --> 00:04:06.602
We typically sought very
long multi-year engagements

00:04:06.602 --> 00:04:08.060
with our clients,
but we're finding

00:04:08.060 --> 00:04:10.580
that was becoming really
unpalatable to a lot

00:04:10.580 --> 00:04:12.580
of our prospects.

00:04:12.580 --> 00:04:15.270
Clients really wanted
shorter engagements with us.

00:04:15.270 --> 00:04:18.339
And they were looking for
a lot more flexibility

00:04:18.339 --> 00:04:20.930
of how we can actually
provide what we provide.

00:04:20.930 --> 00:04:22.810
Our technology stack
was very proprietary.

00:04:22.810 --> 00:04:25.060
It was best of breed, which
is another word for saying

00:04:25.060 --> 00:04:26.680
it was very expensive.

00:04:26.680 --> 00:04:31.090
And we hosted all of our
own infrastructure as well.

00:04:31.090 --> 00:04:33.790
So we have four data centers--
two in the US, two in the UK.

00:04:33.790 --> 00:04:36.130
And we've got about 4
petabytes of data stored

00:04:36.130 --> 00:04:38.330
across those data centers.

00:04:38.330 --> 00:04:40.090
So we made a conscious
decision to move

00:04:40.090 --> 00:04:42.230
to open source and cloud.

00:04:42.230 --> 00:04:45.490
Open source because it was
cheaper, much more flexibility.

00:04:45.490 --> 00:04:48.310
And cloud because buying
hardware for short client

00:04:48.310 --> 00:04:50.860
engagements was never really
going to be feasible for us.

00:04:50.860 --> 00:04:53.800
So we're about halfway through
our journey of migrating

00:04:53.800 --> 00:04:56.020
all our clients to the cloud.

00:04:56.020 --> 00:04:58.000
We hope to in a
couple of years fully

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:00.250
deprecate all of
our data centers.

00:05:00.250 --> 00:05:01.870
The technology stack
that we're using

00:05:01.870 --> 00:05:03.800
is what I've put
on the slide here.

00:05:03.800 --> 00:05:07.300
So we were using Oracle
Exadata for storage,

00:05:07.300 --> 00:05:11.680
SAS for data processing,
and Redwood Cronacle

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:13.870
as our scheduling tool.

00:05:13.870 --> 00:05:16.420
All three of which, best
of breed, very expensive,

00:05:16.420 --> 00:05:19.870
and they cost us a lot of money.

00:05:19.870 --> 00:05:21.330
So we made the--

00:05:21.330 --> 00:05:24.220
through a program of work,
which we call tech renewal,

00:05:24.220 --> 00:05:28.600
we made a lot of changes to how
we deliver stuff and the tools

00:05:28.600 --> 00:05:30.110
that we use to
actually deliver it.

00:05:30.110 --> 00:05:31.810
So we made a big
investment in Python

00:05:31.810 --> 00:05:35.600
as our chosen language
for our data scientists.

00:05:35.600 --> 00:05:38.710
We made a big bet
on Apache Spark.

00:05:38.710 --> 00:05:42.580
We adopted Spark when it
was around the 1.6 version.

00:05:42.580 --> 00:05:46.180
I think since then it's proved
to be the kind of de facto

00:05:46.180 --> 00:05:47.910
tool for big data processing.

00:05:47.910 --> 00:05:52.250
So we're very pleased with
what Spark's doing for us.

00:05:52.250 --> 00:05:56.030
We have adopted Apache
Airflow as a workflow tool.

00:05:56.030 --> 00:05:59.450
For those that don't know, this
is a scheduling workflow tool

00:05:59.450 --> 00:06:02.310
that came out of Airbnb.

00:06:02.310 --> 00:06:03.825
And we use Jupyter Notebooks.

00:06:03.825 --> 00:06:05.790
Our data scientists
use Jupyter Notebooks.

00:06:05.790 --> 00:06:08.310
We provide them too as
part of the infrastructure

00:06:08.310 --> 00:06:10.020
I'm going to demonstrate today.

00:06:10.020 --> 00:06:12.870
And we made a big bet
on Terraform as well.

00:06:12.870 --> 00:06:16.800
Terraform is a tool from a
company called HashiCorp.

00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:19.500
And what it does is allow you
to manage your infrastructure

00:06:19.500 --> 00:06:20.890
as code.

00:06:20.890 --> 00:06:22.832
So we're using that
to deploy everything

00:06:22.832 --> 00:06:24.540
that I'm going to be
talking about today.

00:06:24.540 --> 00:06:27.120
And I've got a demo
a little bit later

00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:29.400
that will talk a
little bit about that.

00:06:29.400 --> 00:06:31.350
We're using Grafana,
another open source

00:06:31.350 --> 00:06:35.990
tool for visualizing
monitoring data.

00:06:35.990 --> 00:06:38.080
And finally, the other
big bet that we made

00:06:38.080 --> 00:06:41.950
was choosing GCP as our
preferred cloud provider.

00:06:41.950 --> 00:06:44.680
And as part of
that, Dataproc has

00:06:44.680 --> 00:06:47.110
become the cornerstone
of everything

00:06:47.110 --> 00:06:48.250
that we're doing on GCP.

00:06:48.250 --> 00:06:50.930
And we've been using that
for about two years now.

00:06:50.930 --> 00:06:52.513
So tech renewal has
provided Dunnhumby

00:06:52.513 --> 00:06:53.722
with quite a lot of benefits.

00:06:53.722 --> 00:06:55.540
We now have a modern
tech stack, the things

00:06:55.540 --> 00:06:56.980
I was just talking about.

00:06:56.980 --> 00:06:59.660
Massive reduction in cost,
mostly through the lack--

00:06:59.660 --> 00:07:02.200
we don't have to pay
license fees anymore.

00:07:02.200 --> 00:07:03.040
Better provisioning.

00:07:03.040 --> 00:07:05.710
We can provision infrastructure
for new clients a lot quicker

00:07:05.710 --> 00:07:08.680
than we could ever
do that in the past.

00:07:08.680 --> 00:07:09.940
Much more scalable platform.

00:07:09.940 --> 00:07:12.580
We can dial it up
and down as we need.

00:07:12.580 --> 00:07:15.010
We have a lot more
hosting options.

00:07:15.010 --> 00:07:16.870
Previously, all we could
do was host clients

00:07:16.870 --> 00:07:17.990
in the US or the UK.

00:07:17.990 --> 00:07:23.380
We can now host them wherever
Google has got a data center.

00:07:23.380 --> 00:07:25.540
Much more robust security.

00:07:25.540 --> 00:07:28.810
We build on top of Google's
investments in that area.

00:07:28.810 --> 00:07:30.760
And we benefit from that.

00:07:30.760 --> 00:07:33.000
We're also finding a lot
more opportunities from this

00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:33.500
as well.

00:07:33.500 --> 00:07:34.930
There's a lot more
clients out there

00:07:34.930 --> 00:07:36.790
who want to be on the
cloud and want to come

00:07:36.790 --> 00:07:37.790
on this journey with us.

00:07:37.790 --> 00:07:40.410
And it's providing a lot more
partnering opportunities for us

00:07:40.410 --> 00:07:40.910
as well.

00:07:43.180 --> 00:07:43.680
OK.

00:07:43.680 --> 00:07:46.650
So that's enough spiel about
what Dunnhumby actually do,

00:07:46.650 --> 00:07:47.620
and what we are.

00:07:47.620 --> 00:07:50.130
I want to talk more now
about the real meat of what

00:07:50.130 --> 00:07:50.630
we're doing.

00:07:50.630 --> 00:07:54.160
And this is something we call
transient Dataproc clusters.

00:07:54.160 --> 00:07:57.477
Google will refer to it as a
ephemeral Dataproc clusters.

00:07:57.477 --> 00:07:59.560
I don't know why we called
it something different,

00:07:59.560 --> 00:08:02.830
but transient data clusters is
how we refer to it internally.

00:08:02.830 --> 00:08:05.620
Basically it means when
we're not using Dataproc,

00:08:05.620 --> 00:08:06.880
we turn it off.

00:08:06.880 --> 00:08:08.440
That's a very simple premise.

00:08:08.440 --> 00:08:12.860
And this presentation really
talks about how we do that.

00:08:12.860 --> 00:08:15.230
So there is a blog post,
the link at the bottom,

00:08:15.230 --> 00:08:16.810
which is from
Google that explains

00:08:16.810 --> 00:08:19.100
exactly how to do this.

00:08:19.100 --> 00:08:23.635
So a flexible way to deploy
Hive on Cloud Dataproc.

00:08:23.635 --> 00:08:25.260
And the way it works
is you can imagine

00:08:25.260 --> 00:08:27.635
you might have multiple clients
that actually want to use

00:08:27.635 --> 00:08:29.758
Hive running upon Dataproc.

00:08:29.758 --> 00:08:31.050
So that might be Spark clients.

00:08:31.050 --> 00:08:33.700
It might be MapReduce clients.

00:08:33.700 --> 00:08:36.179
So in this diagram
here, you'll see

00:08:36.179 --> 00:08:38.820
we have two of these
transient clusters,

00:08:38.820 --> 00:08:41.100
or ephemeral clusters
as Google call them.

00:08:41.100 --> 00:08:44.940
I took this diagram from
the blog post actually.

00:08:44.940 --> 00:08:48.210
And each of those clusters is
running Hive, Hive metastore

00:08:48.210 --> 00:08:48.960
service.

00:08:48.960 --> 00:08:51.420
And it runs a component
called Cloud SQL proxy, which

00:08:51.420 --> 00:08:55.320
is a Google-provided
component that allows the Hive

00:08:55.320 --> 00:08:57.450
service running on
that ephemeral cluster

00:08:57.450 --> 00:08:59.720
to connect to a Hive metastore.

00:08:59.720 --> 00:09:04.790
And that Hive metastore
is running in Cloud SQL,

00:09:04.790 --> 00:09:06.440
Google's Cloud SQL offering.

00:09:06.440 --> 00:09:08.890
Specifically, we use MySQL.

00:09:08.890 --> 00:09:11.810
The data itself that
we process and we store

00:09:11.810 --> 00:09:15.940
is stored in Google Cloud
Storage in storage buckets.

00:09:15.940 --> 00:09:18.100
We don't store the data
on the cluster itself,

00:09:18.100 --> 00:09:20.780
because as I was saying, when
we're not using those clusters,

00:09:20.780 --> 00:09:21.905
we want to get rid of them.

00:09:21.905 --> 00:09:24.440
Obviously, we need
the data to persist.

00:09:24.440 --> 00:09:28.530
So we use Google Cloud Storage
buckets in order to do that.

00:09:28.530 --> 00:09:31.580
So we offer transient
clusters for both

00:09:31.580 --> 00:09:33.693
of those types of
groups of users

00:09:33.693 --> 00:09:34.860
that we talked about before.

00:09:34.860 --> 00:09:36.830
So our data engineers
and data scientists.

00:09:39.360 --> 00:09:42.350
We group all the things that
we deploy into components.

00:09:42.350 --> 00:09:44.210
We have a component
we call data platform.

00:09:44.210 --> 00:09:47.000
It's specifically
what I work on.

00:09:47.000 --> 00:09:49.250
And what we provide
to our data engineers

00:09:49.250 --> 00:09:50.863
are these short-lived clusters.

00:09:50.863 --> 00:09:52.280
They're clusters
that you spend up

00:09:52.280 --> 00:09:54.420
to do a specific piece of work.

00:09:54.420 --> 00:09:58.300
And then you shut down
when you're not using them.

00:09:58.300 --> 00:10:02.110
The way we do that is we
allow our data engineers

00:10:02.110 --> 00:10:05.860
to provision clusters via
a series of custom Airflow

00:10:05.860 --> 00:10:09.890
operators that we
wrote ourselves.

00:10:09.890 --> 00:10:13.180
And so our data engineers
use Airflow as an interface

00:10:13.180 --> 00:10:15.447
to create these clusters and
create their retail jobs,

00:10:15.447 --> 00:10:16.780
and then shut the clusters down.

00:10:20.040 --> 00:10:26.340
By using clusters for a
single use, for a single job,

00:10:26.340 --> 00:10:28.290
that provides a great
advantage to us,

00:10:28.290 --> 00:10:31.410
because we can actually
label those cluster resources

00:10:31.410 --> 00:10:34.410
according to what they are
actually going to be used for.

00:10:34.410 --> 00:10:36.330
So a data engineer
might be doing

00:10:36.330 --> 00:10:38.640
some work around
dimension processing

00:10:38.640 --> 00:10:41.490
or transaction aggregation or
feature generation, something

00:10:41.490 --> 00:10:42.720
like that.

00:10:42.720 --> 00:10:46.375
If we can label what that
cluster is actually used for,

00:10:46.375 --> 00:10:48.000
we can actually get
a fine grained cost

00:10:48.000 --> 00:10:50.572
analysis of how much
it actually cost us

00:10:50.572 --> 00:10:51.780
to carry out those functions.

00:10:51.780 --> 00:10:54.720
And you'll see that
a little bit later.

00:10:54.720 --> 00:10:59.740
Finally, we use a feature
called Scheduled Deletion.

00:10:59.740 --> 00:11:01.505
This is something
that Google provide.

00:11:01.505 --> 00:11:02.130
It's an option.

00:11:02.130 --> 00:11:04.680
When you create a
Dataproc cluster that

00:11:04.680 --> 00:11:08.130
allows you to specify
the amount of time

00:11:08.130 --> 00:11:10.830
that the cluster should
be idle for before Google

00:11:10.830 --> 00:11:14.250
will automatically shut it down
for you, which is really handy.

00:11:14.250 --> 00:11:15.930
Typically, we set
this to 30 minutes.

00:11:15.930 --> 00:11:19.690
I think the minimum time you
can set it to is 10 minutes.

00:11:19.690 --> 00:11:21.690
But basically, as soon
as the cluster is idle--

00:11:21.690 --> 00:11:23.190
it's not being used anymore--

00:11:23.190 --> 00:11:26.090
Google will shut that
down on our behalf.

00:11:26.090 --> 00:11:27.950
And the way they do that--

00:11:27.950 --> 00:11:31.700
so this is the command to
actually create a cluster.

00:11:31.700 --> 00:11:34.460
And there is an option,
which is max idle.

00:11:34.460 --> 00:11:37.203
You set that to whatever
duration you need,

00:11:37.203 --> 00:11:39.620
and it will shut it down for
you when it's not being used.

00:11:39.620 --> 00:11:42.480
You'll see it's in
beta at the moment.

00:11:42.480 --> 00:11:44.250
It's been in beta for
quite a long time.

00:11:44.250 --> 00:11:46.130
But if anyone went
to the session

00:11:46.130 --> 00:11:48.317
yesterday, Chris
Crosby's session,

00:11:48.317 --> 00:11:49.400
somebody asked about this.

00:11:49.400 --> 00:11:51.770
And they basically said,
yes, it's effectively

00:11:51.770 --> 00:11:53.760
been GA for a long time.

00:11:53.760 --> 00:11:56.630
They just haven't got round to
switching off the beta tag yet.

00:11:56.630 --> 00:11:57.550
But it's there.

00:11:57.550 --> 00:11:59.050
We've been using
it for a long time.

00:11:59.050 --> 00:12:00.592
And it's been very
successful for us.

00:12:04.930 --> 00:12:05.430
OK.

00:12:05.430 --> 00:12:07.260
So that was talking
about the clusters

00:12:07.260 --> 00:12:10.650
that we provide to
our data engineers.

00:12:10.650 --> 00:12:12.943
Now I'll talk a little bit
about our data scientists.

00:12:12.943 --> 00:12:15.360
They have a slightly different
need to our data engineers.

00:12:15.360 --> 00:12:18.600
They will need to carry
out ad hoc analyses.

00:12:18.600 --> 00:12:21.330
And for that reason, we always
need to have clusters available

00:12:21.330 --> 00:12:22.740
for them to do their work.

00:12:22.740 --> 00:12:26.500
It takes two or three minutes
to spin up a Dataproc cluster.

00:12:26.500 --> 00:12:29.890
So what we never want to do
is leave our data scientists

00:12:29.890 --> 00:12:31.890
sitting around waiting
for a cluster to spin up.

00:12:31.890 --> 00:12:35.890
We need to give them much
more immediate access to that.

00:12:35.890 --> 00:12:39.390
So what we provide for them
is auto-scaling clusters.

00:12:39.390 --> 00:12:43.230
These are clusters
that will increase--

00:12:43.230 --> 00:12:45.540
the number of nodes will
increase as the load

00:12:45.540 --> 00:12:47.830
on those clusters increases.

00:12:47.830 --> 00:12:49.620
So the more users
that are using it,

00:12:49.620 --> 00:12:51.272
the bigger those
clusters will get.

00:12:51.272 --> 00:12:52.980
We'll talk a little
bit on the next slide

00:12:52.980 --> 00:12:56.510
about how we actually do that.

00:12:56.510 --> 00:12:59.273
We also offer Jupyter.

00:12:59.273 --> 00:13:00.940
So every single one
of our data sciences

00:13:00.940 --> 00:13:04.660
has the ability to visit
a portal that we provide

00:13:04.660 --> 00:13:10.030
and spin up their own dedicated
Jupyter Notebook running

00:13:10.030 --> 00:13:13.580
inside a Kubernetes pod that
we manage on their behalf.

00:13:13.580 --> 00:13:16.660
So there's no need
for a data scientist

00:13:16.660 --> 00:13:18.400
to install anything
on their machine.

00:13:18.400 --> 00:13:19.600
We provide it for them.

00:13:19.600 --> 00:13:21.550
All they need is a
browser that will

00:13:21.550 --> 00:13:23.500
enable them to use Jupyter.

00:13:23.500 --> 00:13:26.530
And of course, then Jupiter
offers Python and R.

00:13:26.530 --> 00:13:28.450
So our data scientists
have the option

00:13:28.450 --> 00:13:32.440
of using Python or R for the
stuff that they're doing.

00:13:32.440 --> 00:13:36.790
So a slide specific to
auto-scaling clusters.

00:13:36.790 --> 00:13:40.530
So you may be aware that
Google have got a feature--

00:13:40.530 --> 00:13:42.290
I think it's in
beta at the moment--

00:13:42.290 --> 00:13:43.660
an auto-scaling feature.

00:13:43.660 --> 00:13:45.850
We're not actually using that.

00:13:45.850 --> 00:13:47.830
We actually built
our own tool, which

00:13:47.830 --> 00:13:52.860
we call Tidebell, which will
actually provide auto-scaling

00:13:52.860 --> 00:13:53.740
for us.

00:13:53.740 --> 00:13:55.580
And the way it
works is it monitors

00:13:55.580 --> 00:14:01.320
cluster utilization via the
YARN API that every cluster has.

00:14:01.320 --> 00:14:04.670
And if it notices that
memory pressure is getting

00:14:04.670 --> 00:14:06.170
above a certain
threshold, it will

00:14:06.170 --> 00:14:11.870
start to add more nodes into
that cluster dynamically.

00:14:11.870 --> 00:14:14.210
It uses preemptible
nodes to do that.

00:14:14.210 --> 00:14:15.700
What are preemptible nodes?

00:14:15.700 --> 00:14:19.050
Well, Dataproc is built
upon Google's Compute Engine

00:14:19.050 --> 00:14:20.205
service.

00:14:20.205 --> 00:14:22.080
And they've got an
offering on Compute Engine

00:14:22.080 --> 00:14:23.970
called preemptible machines.

00:14:23.970 --> 00:14:29.820
And what they are,
they're standard VMs.

00:14:29.820 --> 00:14:33.990
However, they do--
they allow Google,

00:14:33.990 --> 00:14:36.510
when they need data
center capacity,

00:14:36.510 --> 00:14:39.300
to take that away from what
they're being used for.

00:14:39.300 --> 00:14:42.125
So Google will actually
take those down away from us

00:14:42.125 --> 00:14:43.500
with about a five
minute warning.

00:14:46.030 --> 00:14:48.130
But that means they're
really well scheduled

00:14:48.130 --> 00:14:52.420
to systems which are resilient
to clusters going away.

00:14:52.420 --> 00:14:54.650
And Spark is really
resilient to that.

00:14:54.650 --> 00:14:57.985
So it's great for us to use
these preemptible nodes.

00:14:57.985 --> 00:14:59.610
The main benefit is,
obviously, they're

00:14:59.610 --> 00:15:00.830
a heck of a lot cheaper.

00:15:00.830 --> 00:15:03.340
So I saw on a slide yesterday
from the Dataproc team

00:15:03.340 --> 00:15:05.200
that you can get up
to 80% cost savings

00:15:05.200 --> 00:15:07.190
just through using
preemptible nodes.

00:15:07.190 --> 00:15:10.240
So that's pretty much
why we want to use them.

00:15:10.240 --> 00:15:14.650
We do expect to replace
Tidebell in the very

00:15:14.650 --> 00:15:18.820
near future with Dataproc's
own auto-scaling feature.

00:15:18.820 --> 00:15:20.890
That's currently in beta.

00:15:20.890 --> 00:15:24.730
And aside from that, I think
last week they also announced

00:15:24.730 --> 00:15:26.140
a new feature,
which is currently

00:15:26.140 --> 00:15:31.210
in alpha, which is called
Enhanced Flexible Mode.

00:15:31.210 --> 00:15:33.970
I think it is EFM.

00:15:33.970 --> 00:15:35.680
And what that will
allow you to do

00:15:35.680 --> 00:15:38.920
is make it a lot easier
to use preemptible nodes,

00:15:38.920 --> 00:15:41.950
because what it actually does
is persist the state of the jobs

00:15:41.950 --> 00:15:45.990
to HDFS before those nodes
actually get shut down.

00:15:45.990 --> 00:15:50.687
So really, we do have some
issues with preemptible nodes.

00:15:50.687 --> 00:15:53.020
Obviously, if a node's going
away when a job is running,

00:15:53.020 --> 00:15:55.390
that's not great.

00:15:55.390 --> 00:15:57.990
But this new EFM features should
be really valuable for us.

00:15:57.990 --> 00:15:59.990
I'm actually seeing Mikayla
in the back nodding,

00:15:59.990 --> 00:16:02.365
so I think she's-- if you want
to know anything about it,

00:16:02.365 --> 00:16:05.970
Mikayla in the back knows a
lot more about it than I do.

00:16:05.970 --> 00:16:09.780
So I said we use Grafana for
visualizing monitoring data.

00:16:09.780 --> 00:16:12.060
And this is a
screenshot from Grafana.

00:16:12.060 --> 00:16:17.190
So this shows activity
across our whole state,

00:16:17.190 --> 00:16:19.360
across our Dataproc state--

00:16:19.360 --> 00:16:21.660
pardon me-- over
a 24-hour period.

00:16:21.660 --> 00:16:24.870
What we're looking at
here is the blue line

00:16:24.870 --> 00:16:27.120
is the number of
nodes that we actually

00:16:27.120 --> 00:16:31.600
have active on Dataproc at
a particular point in time.

00:16:31.600 --> 00:16:34.060
So you see when we actually
took this screenshot,

00:16:34.060 --> 00:16:40.210
we had 105 nodes currently
active at that time.

00:16:40.210 --> 00:16:42.340
The yellow line is the
number of primary workers.

00:16:42.340 --> 00:16:45.730
That means the non-preemptible
nodes that we have running.

00:16:45.730 --> 00:16:48.430
And the red shade
there at the bottom

00:16:48.430 --> 00:16:50.990
is the number of preemptions
that actually occur.

00:16:50.990 --> 00:16:52.840
So this is the number
of times in a day

00:16:52.840 --> 00:16:57.892
that Google will take compute
resources away from us.

00:16:57.892 --> 00:16:59.350
So I just want to
talk a little bit

00:16:59.350 --> 00:17:01.780
about how our data
scientists actually

00:17:01.780 --> 00:17:05.380
work on this infrastructure.

00:17:05.380 --> 00:17:09.329
So typically, a data scientist
will require the ability

00:17:09.329 --> 00:17:12.599
to do ad hoc analysis-- so
carrying out investigations

00:17:12.599 --> 00:17:15.240
upon the data that we provide
in that common data model

00:17:15.240 --> 00:17:16.619
I talked about earlier.

00:17:16.619 --> 00:17:20.150
They'll do that upon our
auto-scaling clusters,

00:17:20.150 --> 00:17:22.200
because that's actually
why to provide them.

00:17:22.200 --> 00:17:24.200
And that's their
reason for existence.

00:17:24.200 --> 00:17:27.064
And we also encourage
our data scientists

00:17:27.064 --> 00:17:30.110
to use sample data sets
rather than full data sets

00:17:30.110 --> 00:17:33.710
for doing exploratory work
for model training and things

00:17:33.710 --> 00:17:35.170
like that.

00:17:35.170 --> 00:17:38.380
The reason being that if
they're using full data

00:17:38.380 --> 00:17:40.978
sets, A, it's going
to cost us more,

00:17:40.978 --> 00:17:42.520
because we're going
to be spinning up

00:17:42.520 --> 00:17:43.790
more nodes to do it.

00:17:43.790 --> 00:17:50.110
And we do have a danger that if
many data scientists are using

00:17:50.110 --> 00:17:53.980
a cluster at any one time, it
is possible for a particular job

00:17:53.980 --> 00:17:56.570
to max out that cluster.

00:17:56.570 --> 00:17:58.540
And that will impact
anybody else using it.

00:17:58.540 --> 00:18:00.500
So we do encourage
them to use sample data

00:18:00.500 --> 00:18:03.010
sets wherever possible.

00:18:03.010 --> 00:18:05.260
So when they've done that,
they've done a lot of work.

00:18:05.260 --> 00:18:08.890
They decide on some work
that they need to do.

00:18:08.890 --> 00:18:10.390
We would then
encourage them to take

00:18:10.390 --> 00:18:13.450
that work they've
done in Jupyter

00:18:13.450 --> 00:18:15.580
and turn that into
a job that can

00:18:15.580 --> 00:18:17.380
be run on one of the
short-lived clusters

00:18:17.380 --> 00:18:18.860
that I talked about earlier.

00:18:18.860 --> 00:18:21.350
So the difference between
our short-lived clusters,

00:18:21.350 --> 00:18:22.960
which have the
scheduled deletion

00:18:22.960 --> 00:18:26.540
option, and our long-lived
clusters that use auto-scaling.

00:18:26.540 --> 00:18:29.590
So they'll use the auto-scaling
clusters for investigation,

00:18:29.590 --> 00:18:32.110
and they'll run
production-ready jobs

00:18:32.110 --> 00:18:34.710
upon our short-lived clusters.

00:18:34.710 --> 00:18:40.130
So what we're looking
at here is a report

00:18:40.130 --> 00:18:42.440
that we built in
Google Data Studio.

00:18:42.440 --> 00:18:45.255
So this sits on top
of our billing data

00:18:45.255 --> 00:18:46.380
that Google provides to us.

00:18:46.380 --> 00:18:50.195
And that billing data
is stored in BigQuery.

00:18:50.195 --> 00:18:51.570
So a number of
things I just want

00:18:51.570 --> 00:18:53.670
to draw your attention to here.

00:18:53.670 --> 00:18:55.740
When we actually use
these Dataproc clusters,

00:18:55.740 --> 00:18:58.590
we use labeling
upon those clusters

00:18:58.590 --> 00:19:00.090
to tell us various
things about what

00:19:00.090 --> 00:19:01.420
they are going to be used for.

00:19:01.420 --> 00:19:05.130
So for example, the data-- we
can slice and dice this data

00:19:05.130 --> 00:19:09.255
by the client, the date that
the cluster is being used for,

00:19:09.255 --> 00:19:10.920
because every
cluster is dedicated

00:19:10.920 --> 00:19:14.120
to a particular client.

00:19:14.120 --> 00:19:17.160
The project that
it's been used in.

00:19:17.160 --> 00:19:18.130
The components.

00:19:18.130 --> 00:19:19.860
Now, what do I
mean by component?

00:19:19.860 --> 00:19:22.710
So I talked earlier about
how we have our data platform

00:19:22.710 --> 00:19:24.630
component that we offer
to data engineers.

00:19:24.630 --> 00:19:26.460
We have our analyst
platform that we

00:19:26.460 --> 00:19:28.330
offer to data scientists.

00:19:28.330 --> 00:19:32.900
So we can slice and dice the
data by those two things.

00:19:32.900 --> 00:19:34.930
We can filter and slice
and dice by the machine

00:19:34.930 --> 00:19:36.650
type and the number of nodes.

00:19:36.650 --> 00:19:40.150
So this is really useful,
because we can actually see--

00:19:40.150 --> 00:19:45.040
we allow our data engineers
to know the cost of actually

00:19:45.040 --> 00:19:46.610
running specific jobs.

00:19:46.610 --> 00:19:50.212
And they can test that using
different node configurations

00:19:50.212 --> 00:19:51.670
and see whether
it's cost effective

00:19:51.670 --> 00:19:56.842
or not to actually use
more nodes in that cluster.

00:19:56.842 --> 00:20:00.040
And finally, this is the
real useful part of it.

00:20:00.040 --> 00:20:04.240
What we've allowed people to do
is label the specific workload

00:20:04.240 --> 00:20:06.350
that they're using
that cluster for.

00:20:06.350 --> 00:20:08.200
So as I talked about
before, a data engineer

00:20:08.200 --> 00:20:11.320
might be doing things like
damage and processing,

00:20:11.320 --> 00:20:15.620
or transaction aggregation,
or feature generation.

00:20:15.620 --> 00:20:18.530
And if they actually
label that cluster--

00:20:18.530 --> 00:20:21.400
we give them tools
to actually do that--

00:20:21.400 --> 00:20:25.710
we can actually get
very specific costings

00:20:25.710 --> 00:20:28.380
about how much it costs
to actually run that job.

00:20:28.380 --> 00:20:32.920
So you see in this
example I'm filtering on--

00:20:32.920 --> 00:20:35.550
I used the word
workload earlier.

00:20:35.550 --> 00:20:37.590
We actually call it a purpose.

00:20:37.590 --> 00:20:40.110
So filtering on a
purpose, a smoke test.

00:20:40.110 --> 00:20:42.780
So this is a test that we
run across our whole state.

00:20:42.780 --> 00:20:44.530
What it does is very simple.

00:20:44.530 --> 00:20:46.300
It spins up a Dataproc cluster.

00:20:46.300 --> 00:20:47.550
It runs some jobs upon it.

00:20:47.550 --> 00:20:49.850
And then it shuts the
cluster down after.

00:20:49.850 --> 00:20:53.880
So we can actually get a
cost for how much it actually

00:20:53.880 --> 00:20:57.460
costs us to actually run
one of those smoke tests.

00:20:57.460 --> 00:20:59.310
So there's an example here.

00:20:59.310 --> 00:21:03.060
You can see it actually costs
us $0.18 every time-- well,

00:21:03.060 --> 00:21:04.098
roughly $0.18--

00:21:04.098 --> 00:21:06.390
every time we spend up a
cluster, run a couple of jobs,

00:21:06.390 --> 00:21:07.483
and shut it down.

00:21:07.483 --> 00:21:09.900
So that sort of fine grained
analysis is fantastic for us.

00:21:09.900 --> 00:21:12.060
We could never do that
when we were running Hadoop

00:21:12.060 --> 00:21:13.860
in our own data
center, because it

00:21:13.860 --> 00:21:20.070
was shared between multiple
clients, multiple engineers.

00:21:20.070 --> 00:21:23.800
Here we're actually having
single use clusters.

00:21:23.800 --> 00:21:26.640
And we can actually get the cost
of spinning one of those up,

00:21:26.640 --> 00:21:28.957
which is really
advantageous for us.

00:21:28.957 --> 00:21:30.665
And you can see over
the past few months,

00:21:30.665 --> 00:21:32.498
if you look in the
bottom right-hand corner,

00:21:32.498 --> 00:21:35.550
that's cost us about $142
over the past five years--

00:21:35.550 --> 00:21:39.960
past five months, just running
that particular smoke test.

00:21:39.960 --> 00:21:45.520
So in a single day,
what do we actually do?

00:21:45.520 --> 00:21:49.450
So look at the lower
numbers, first of all.

00:21:49.450 --> 00:21:54.610
We run these services
for 35 clients currently.

00:21:54.610 --> 00:21:56.350
That's about half
of our client base.

00:21:56.350 --> 00:21:59.180
I said we're about halfway
through our cloud migration.

00:21:59.180 --> 00:22:01.570
So we've got about
35 clients running

00:22:01.570 --> 00:22:06.220
in GCP and approximately
300 terabytes stored

00:22:06.220 --> 00:22:08.680
for those clients.

00:22:08.680 --> 00:22:13.720
On any particular day we
use about 17,000 hours

00:22:13.720 --> 00:22:15.740
of Compute Engine time.

00:22:15.740 --> 00:22:19.690
Our particular hour is using
a core and a Compute Engine

00:22:19.690 --> 00:22:20.950
instance for one hour.

00:22:20.950 --> 00:22:26.920
So 17,000 plus hours
in a particular day.

00:22:26.920 --> 00:22:29.350
Thanks to our auto-scaling
feature, and thanks

00:22:29.350 --> 00:22:31.990
to our short-lived clusters,
which we encourage our data

00:22:31.990 --> 00:22:35.200
engineers to spin up as and
when they need, we actually,

00:22:35.200 --> 00:22:36.880
on the particular
day we measured this,

00:22:36.880 --> 00:22:40.320
we created 2,971 nodes.

00:22:40.320 --> 00:22:42.370
That's physical-- not physical.

00:22:42.370 --> 00:22:44.860
Virtual machines
running Compute Engine

00:22:44.860 --> 00:22:49.430
being used for the
purposes of Dataproc.

00:22:49.430 --> 00:22:52.277
525 of them got preempted.

00:22:52.277 --> 00:22:54.860
So that gives you some sort of
indication about the preemption

00:22:54.860 --> 00:22:57.740
rates that we're
seeing from using

00:22:57.740 --> 00:22:59.660
preemptible nodes on
our Dataproc clusters,

00:22:59.660 --> 00:23:02.060
on our auto-scale clusters.

00:23:02.060 --> 00:23:03.290
So that whole lot--

00:23:03.290 --> 00:23:07.790
17,000 hours costs us
approximately $7,600 a day.

00:23:07.790 --> 00:23:10.392
I'll let you figure out whether
that's expensive or not.

00:23:10.392 --> 00:23:12.350
We're pretty happy with
what that's costing us,

00:23:12.350 --> 00:23:16.430
because as I say, that cost is
spread across those 35 clients.

00:23:16.430 --> 00:23:20.600
And we have approximately
175 data scientists

00:23:20.600 --> 00:23:23.690
on any particular
day using Jupyter

00:23:23.690 --> 00:23:25.370
on our short-lived
clusters accessing

00:23:25.370 --> 00:23:27.745
that data that's sitting in
Google Cloud Storage buckets.

00:23:32.350 --> 00:23:36.770
So this is the money slide
in more ways than one.

00:23:36.770 --> 00:23:42.020
This depicts the cost of
actually providing the services

00:23:42.020 --> 00:23:45.530
that we provide to
just one single client

00:23:45.530 --> 00:23:48.367
over a 12-month period.

00:23:48.367 --> 00:23:50.950
So I didn't include the y-axis,
because the numbers themselves

00:23:50.950 --> 00:23:51.940
are not that important.

00:23:51.940 --> 00:23:54.970
But I think you see the
profile is what I really

00:23:54.970 --> 00:23:56.380
want to draw your attention to.

00:23:56.380 --> 00:23:58.450
And you'll see in
August last year

00:23:58.450 --> 00:24:04.210
we had a huge downturn in the
actual expenditure of cost

00:24:04.210 --> 00:24:05.330
to service client.

00:24:05.330 --> 00:24:07.630
And the reason for
that is very simply

00:24:07.630 --> 00:24:10.120
that's when we introduced
transient clusters

00:24:10.120 --> 00:24:11.180
for this client.

00:24:11.180 --> 00:24:14.500
So a very real
depiction of the cost

00:24:14.500 --> 00:24:17.775
saving that we're getting
from using transient clusters.

00:24:17.775 --> 00:24:19.400
The spiking workloads
that you see over

00:24:19.400 --> 00:24:22.690
to the right-hand side that have
increased over the past three

00:24:22.690 --> 00:24:24.523
or four months, that's
because our analysts,

00:24:24.523 --> 00:24:26.232
our data scientists
are now get much more

00:24:26.232 --> 00:24:27.460
familiar with the platform.

00:24:27.460 --> 00:24:31.210
And they're starting to run much
more production ready workloads

00:24:31.210 --> 00:24:31.840
on this.

00:24:31.840 --> 00:24:34.555
And it's spiky because
we're encouraging

00:24:34.555 --> 00:24:36.430
them to use those
short-lived clusters that I

00:24:36.430 --> 00:24:37.707
talked about earlier on.

00:24:43.320 --> 00:24:46.120
So a lot of people
ask, OK, that's great.

00:24:46.120 --> 00:24:48.690
You're using Dataproc
for things like that.

00:24:48.690 --> 00:24:50.880
It seems to be
pretty successful.

00:24:50.880 --> 00:24:52.880
But you're on GCP.

00:24:52.880 --> 00:24:54.718
Why not just use BigQuery?

00:24:54.718 --> 00:24:57.260
I've had a lot of people from
Google asking me the same thing

00:24:57.260 --> 00:24:59.250
this week as well.

00:24:59.250 --> 00:25:01.340
So there's a few reasons.

00:25:01.340 --> 00:25:04.230
GCP is our preferred
cloud provider,

00:25:04.230 --> 00:25:06.000
but that doesn't mean
we're always going

00:25:06.000 --> 00:25:09.110
to be running stuff on GCP.

00:25:09.110 --> 00:25:10.380
It's not available everywhere.

00:25:10.380 --> 00:25:13.570
We do have clients in
China and in Russia.

00:25:13.570 --> 00:25:15.600
And we do find in
some certain cases

00:25:15.600 --> 00:25:18.300
that our clients may
want to dictate the cloud

00:25:18.300 --> 00:25:19.070
provider to us.

00:25:19.070 --> 00:25:20.790
Some people we've
found are actually not

00:25:20.790 --> 00:25:22.810
happy we're on GCP.

00:25:22.810 --> 00:25:27.400
And in those cases, we have to
provide a different provider.

00:25:27.400 --> 00:25:30.350
So basically, we strive
to be cloud portable,

00:25:30.350 --> 00:25:31.873
but we're not cloud agnostic.

00:25:31.873 --> 00:25:33.540
We want to be able
to move our workloads

00:25:33.540 --> 00:25:37.050
across different clouds.

00:25:37.050 --> 00:25:39.270
And we're making a bet on
open source technologies

00:25:39.270 --> 00:25:40.920
like Hadoop.

00:25:40.920 --> 00:25:43.530
And Spark has been
a driver of that.

00:25:43.530 --> 00:25:45.690
And our other big bet that
we've made technically

00:25:45.690 --> 00:25:47.910
has been around
Kubernetes as well.

00:25:47.910 --> 00:25:50.532
So adopting those open
source technologies hopefully

00:25:50.532 --> 00:25:51.990
will allow us to
move our workflows

00:25:51.990 --> 00:25:53.780
across different
clouds in the future.

00:25:53.780 --> 00:25:56.490
And obviously, the Anthos
announcement a couple of days

00:25:56.490 --> 00:26:00.480
ago is going to be
very interesting to us.

00:26:00.480 --> 00:26:03.030
All that said, we do use
BigQuery for some things.

00:26:03.030 --> 00:26:05.540
I talked about how we use
it for our billing data.

00:26:05.540 --> 00:26:08.410
That drives the report you
saw a couple of slides back.

00:26:08.410 --> 00:26:10.975
We also have some
direct-to-consumer offerings

00:26:10.975 --> 00:26:11.850
that we have as well.

00:26:11.850 --> 00:26:14.940
So we have a receipt scanning
mobile app called HuYu.

00:26:14.940 --> 00:26:17.290
It's only in the
UK at the moment,

00:26:17.290 --> 00:26:21.840
but basically it
provides loyalty rewards

00:26:21.840 --> 00:26:24.480
to customers who want to
actually scan their receipts.

00:26:24.480 --> 00:26:27.270
And all the data for that gets
stored in BigQuery as well.

00:26:27.270 --> 00:26:29.460
Because that's a
direct-to-consumer offering,

00:26:29.460 --> 00:26:32.680
it's essentially the client
is Dunnhumby ourselves.

00:26:32.680 --> 00:26:34.830
So we can host that
wherever we want, and we

00:26:34.830 --> 00:26:36.628
choose to put that in BigQuery.

00:26:41.270 --> 00:26:43.810
I want to talk a little bit
about the solution architecture

00:26:43.810 --> 00:26:45.700
that we have within
our core technology.

00:26:45.700 --> 00:26:47.920
This is essentially
the architecture

00:26:47.920 --> 00:26:52.140
that we deploy for
each of our clients.

00:26:52.140 --> 00:26:54.430
So the base component is
something we call network.

00:26:54.430 --> 00:27:00.600
That basically consists of
VPC network and Cloud DNS.

00:27:00.600 --> 00:27:04.080
So we provision DNS
A records and CNAME

00:27:04.080 --> 00:27:08.550
records for that client.

00:27:08.550 --> 00:27:11.220
On top of that, we deploy
a component we call base.

00:27:11.220 --> 00:27:14.760
This provides an
external proxy so

00:27:14.760 --> 00:27:17.480
that we can get to the outside
world should we need to.

00:27:17.480 --> 00:27:20.850
An SSH gateway so that we
as engineers can actually

00:27:20.850 --> 00:27:23.550
get onto the
infrastructure itself

00:27:23.550 --> 00:27:27.090
should we need to for
investigation, for maintenance,

00:27:27.090 --> 00:27:28.480
anything like that.

00:27:28.480 --> 00:27:31.800
And we also provide a single
sign-on authentication proxy

00:27:31.800 --> 00:27:33.430
as well.

00:27:33.430 --> 00:27:36.712
That's all part of
our base components.

00:27:36.712 --> 00:27:38.170
On top of that, we
have a component

00:27:38.170 --> 00:27:39.460
we call Hive metastore.

00:27:39.460 --> 00:27:41.543
And this is what I was
talking about a bit earlier

00:27:41.543 --> 00:27:44.290
in the talk where we
use Google's Cloud SQL

00:27:44.290 --> 00:27:46.780
offering to host the
Hive metastore that

00:27:46.780 --> 00:27:50.010
will get used by our
transient ephemeral clusters.

00:27:50.010 --> 00:27:51.890
So that is a component
we deploy on its own.

00:27:51.890 --> 00:27:57.100
It essentially is a MySQL
database running in Cloud SQL.

00:27:57.100 --> 00:28:00.110
On top of that, we have our
data platform component,

00:28:00.110 --> 00:28:03.040
which is the thing
I said I worked on.

00:28:03.040 --> 00:28:05.170
This is where we provide
short-lived clusters

00:28:05.170 --> 00:28:06.550
to our data engineers.

00:28:06.550 --> 00:28:11.740
We do that through a
combination of Dataproc,

00:28:11.740 --> 00:28:13.690
storage buckets, Airflow.

00:28:13.690 --> 00:28:16.690
And we also provide Jupyter
instances to our data engineers

00:28:16.690 --> 00:28:19.190
as well.

00:28:19.190 --> 00:28:21.260
On top of that, our
analyst platform

00:28:21.260 --> 00:28:25.130
I talked about before, again,
we're using transient clusters.

00:28:25.130 --> 00:28:27.710
We're using-- our
also-scaled clusters

00:28:27.710 --> 00:28:29.270
using our Tidebell tool.

00:28:29.270 --> 00:28:33.860
And we provide Jupyter
running on top of Kubernetes

00:28:33.860 --> 00:28:36.030
to our data scientists as well.

00:28:36.030 --> 00:28:39.245
We also run our various
products on top of this as well.

00:28:43.210 --> 00:28:46.200
So that's our architecture.

00:28:46.200 --> 00:28:47.900
The way we actually
deploy that is

00:28:47.900 --> 00:28:51.560
across two separate projects,
two separate GCP projects.

00:28:51.560 --> 00:28:54.080
We have a project
dedicated for hosting

00:28:54.080 --> 00:28:57.650
our runtime infrastructure
and our projects dedicated

00:28:57.650 --> 00:28:59.600
to hosting data essentially.

00:28:59.600 --> 00:29:00.770
So a state.

00:29:00.770 --> 00:29:02.320
So why do we do that?

00:29:02.320 --> 00:29:06.770
It prevents-- it provides quite
a lot of advantages actually.

00:29:06.770 --> 00:29:10.070
So we could keep our long-lived
state in a dedicated project

00:29:10.070 --> 00:29:13.580
and keep our short-lived
runtime in a separate project.

00:29:13.580 --> 00:29:15.823
And really, the key to
this is segregation.

00:29:15.823 --> 00:29:17.240
So it's segregation
of permissions

00:29:17.240 --> 00:29:23.240
around IAM, segregation of data
retention privacy policies,

00:29:23.240 --> 00:29:26.750
and it also makes it easier
for disaster recovery as well.

00:29:30.030 --> 00:29:32.820
So essentially, we
take the architecture

00:29:32.820 --> 00:29:34.630
that I showed a
couple of slides back,

00:29:34.630 --> 00:29:37.100
and we deploy that
across those two projects

00:29:37.100 --> 00:29:39.420
that we dedicate per client.

00:29:39.420 --> 00:29:43.840
So essentially, we have multiple
instances of this architecture

00:29:43.840 --> 00:29:46.090
running across our GCP state.

00:29:49.840 --> 00:29:50.680
So why do we do it?

00:29:50.680 --> 00:29:56.333
Why do we have this single
tenant deployment model?

00:29:56.333 --> 00:29:58.250
Previously, when we run
everything on-premises

00:29:58.250 --> 00:30:00.500
in our own data center, we
didn't have a single tenant

00:30:00.500 --> 00:30:01.280
deployment model.

00:30:01.280 --> 00:30:02.820
We had very big clusters.

00:30:02.820 --> 00:30:05.990
And we shared them
between multiple clients.

00:30:05.990 --> 00:30:07.850
Now we're in the cloud.

00:30:07.850 --> 00:30:09.960
We can use a single
deployment model.

00:30:09.960 --> 00:30:12.705
And the reason really is
one of segregation, really.

00:30:12.705 --> 00:30:14.330
It's keeping things
separate that don't

00:30:14.330 --> 00:30:16.730
need to be in the same place.

00:30:16.730 --> 00:30:18.680
It's a lot simpler for
us to assign access

00:30:18.680 --> 00:30:20.900
for a client to actually
access our infrastructure.

00:30:20.900 --> 00:30:24.730
We can just control that
with the natural boundary

00:30:24.730 --> 00:30:27.460
that GCP project provides.

00:30:27.460 --> 00:30:29.190
It's a lot simpler
for us to operate.

00:30:29.190 --> 00:30:31.280
We don't need to
worry about using

00:30:31.280 --> 00:30:33.860
Kerberos or anything like that.

00:30:33.860 --> 00:30:36.080
Cost transparency is
a big factor for us.

00:30:36.080 --> 00:30:38.100
If we want to actually
see the cost of serving

00:30:38.100 --> 00:30:40.130
a particular client, it's
very easy to do that,

00:30:40.130 --> 00:30:42.560
because the billing data
can all be grouped up

00:30:42.560 --> 00:30:44.720
to a particular project,
or a couple of projects.

00:30:44.720 --> 00:30:47.322
So we can see very
easily the cost

00:30:47.322 --> 00:30:48.530
of actually serving a client.

00:30:48.530 --> 00:30:52.740
And you saw that on
an earlier slide.

00:30:52.740 --> 00:30:54.960
The other big advantage
that it provides

00:30:54.960 --> 00:30:57.480
is the ability to
scale from zero.

00:30:57.480 --> 00:30:59.080
So what do I mean by that?

00:30:59.080 --> 00:31:01.440
If you think about
how we work, we

00:31:01.440 --> 00:31:05.520
will sign a client to
do some work for them.

00:31:05.520 --> 00:31:07.883
We'll provision two
projects on their behalf.

00:31:07.883 --> 00:31:09.300
At that point,
there's essentially

00:31:09.300 --> 00:31:10.450
nothing in those projects.

00:31:10.450 --> 00:31:11.670
There's no data.

00:31:11.670 --> 00:31:13.270
There's no runtime components.

00:31:13.270 --> 00:31:15.930
And none of our data
scientists or data engineers

00:31:15.930 --> 00:31:17.320
will actually have access to it.

00:31:17.320 --> 00:31:18.810
So literally, we start.

00:31:18.810 --> 00:31:19.660
We have nothing.

00:31:19.660 --> 00:31:24.060
It makes it a lot easier
to scale that up as

00:31:24.060 --> 00:31:25.920
and when we need.

00:31:25.920 --> 00:31:28.430
We never had the
capability to do that when

00:31:28.430 --> 00:31:31.440
we were on-premises because
we were using share clusters.

00:31:31.440 --> 00:31:33.210
So we had to provision
massive clusters

00:31:33.210 --> 00:31:35.010
in advance of actually
signing our clients

00:31:35.010 --> 00:31:37.440
before we actually know that
we were going to have them.

00:31:37.440 --> 00:31:41.500
So it makes it a lot easier for
us to provide infrastructure

00:31:41.500 --> 00:31:42.375
to brand new clients.

00:31:48.080 --> 00:31:49.790
That's really the
meat of the talk.

00:31:49.790 --> 00:31:52.820
I've been talking about
transient clusters,

00:31:52.820 --> 00:31:56.060
auto-scale clusters,
and the architecture

00:31:56.060 --> 00:31:58.880
that we use to provide
that to our clients.

00:31:58.880 --> 00:32:03.470
Now, what I've done is
provide a project on GitHub

00:32:03.470 --> 00:32:07.330
that really demonstrates
a lot of how we do this.

00:32:07.330 --> 00:32:10.380
So what this is is a project
that will use Terraform code

00:32:10.380 --> 00:32:13.350
to deploy the parts
of the architecture

00:32:13.350 --> 00:32:15.210
that I just demonstrated.

00:32:15.210 --> 00:32:17.250
And specifically,
what it will do

00:32:17.250 --> 00:32:19.860
is deploy those transient
clusters for us.

00:32:22.943 --> 00:32:24.610
So this is a screenshot
from the Read Me

00:32:24.610 --> 00:32:25.977
that's part of that project.

00:32:25.977 --> 00:32:28.310
Essentially, if you want to
run this, all you need to do

00:32:28.310 --> 00:32:31.670
is clone the repo,
run G Cloud in it,

00:32:31.670 --> 00:32:34.070
which will establish which
projects you actually

00:32:34.070 --> 00:32:36.820
want to-- which GCP projects
you want to deploy this into.

00:32:36.820 --> 00:32:40.450
You enable a couple of services.

00:32:40.450 --> 00:32:41.680
Specify the project.

00:32:41.680 --> 00:32:44.650
You have to create
a Cloud Storage

00:32:44.650 --> 00:32:48.400
bucket, which is what Terraform
will use to store its state.

00:32:48.400 --> 00:32:50.350
So Terraform needs
to keep a record

00:32:50.350 --> 00:32:51.640
of what has been deployed.

00:32:51.640 --> 00:32:55.420
It does that in
a storage bucket.

00:32:55.420 --> 00:32:57.220
And then there's a
makefile in there which

00:32:57.220 --> 00:32:58.690
will actually show you how to--

00:32:58.690 --> 00:33:00.830
which will actually deploy
this on your behalf.

00:33:00.830 --> 00:33:07.120
So you could clone that, create
a project, run the apply.

00:33:07.120 --> 00:33:09.120
That whole thing should
take about five minutes.

00:33:09.120 --> 00:33:10.787
I don't want to demo
it today because we

00:33:10.787 --> 00:33:13.370
don't want to stare at a command
line for about five minutes.

00:33:13.370 --> 00:33:15.160
But if you want to
see how this works

00:33:15.160 --> 00:33:16.832
and how we set this
up using Terraform,

00:33:16.832 --> 00:33:18.290
this is a really
good way to do it.

00:33:18.290 --> 00:33:19.920
And when you're finished you
could just run make destroy,

00:33:19.920 --> 00:33:21.670
and it'll tear the
whole thing down again.

00:33:24.800 --> 00:33:26.270
One thing you can
do with Terraform

00:33:26.270 --> 00:33:28.550
is actually visualize
all the components

00:33:28.550 --> 00:33:30.150
that it's going
to deploy for you.

00:33:30.150 --> 00:33:32.400
And this is the output of
running that Terraform graph

00:33:32.400 --> 00:33:33.210
command.

00:33:33.210 --> 00:33:35.002
The command in the
bottom right-hand corner

00:33:35.002 --> 00:33:37.310
shows you exactly what
command you need to run,

00:33:37.310 --> 00:33:39.790
which will generate
this graph for you.

00:33:39.790 --> 00:33:43.520
And just to call out
a few things on here,

00:33:43.520 --> 00:33:47.813
the yellow boxes are
the network components

00:33:47.813 --> 00:33:48.980
that it will deploy for you.

00:33:48.980 --> 00:33:51.917
So we deploy a network, a sub
network, and various firewalls

00:33:51.917 --> 00:33:53.750
that enable the different
components to talk

00:33:53.750 --> 00:33:55.640
to each other.

00:33:55.640 --> 00:33:58.700
We deploy a Google
storage bucket which

00:33:58.700 --> 00:34:00.830
will actually store the data.

00:34:00.830 --> 00:34:04.940
That's the purple component
you can see at the bottom.

00:34:04.940 --> 00:34:07.490
The red components are
the Hive metastore.

00:34:07.490 --> 00:34:09.830
That's for the Cloud
SQL instance that

00:34:09.830 --> 00:34:11.810
is hosting the Hive metastore.

00:34:11.810 --> 00:34:15.080
And a SQL use enables
us to access that.

00:34:15.080 --> 00:34:18.560
The blue are the ephemeral
clusters themselves,

00:34:18.560 --> 00:34:19.610
the transient clusters.

00:34:19.610 --> 00:34:22.070
We create-- this project
will create two of them

00:34:22.070 --> 00:34:25.514
for you, both of them
pointing at that shared Hive

00:34:25.514 --> 00:34:27.889
metastore, which really is
the crux of this presentation,

00:34:27.889 --> 00:34:29.090
to be honest.

00:34:29.090 --> 00:34:31.179
And then on top of
that, the green boxes

00:34:31.179 --> 00:34:34.290
are some jobs that we run
on top of those clusters.

00:34:34.290 --> 00:34:36.469
And as I say, deploying
this whole thing

00:34:36.469 --> 00:34:38.600
and running those jobs
takes four or five minutes.

00:34:38.600 --> 00:34:40.650
Then you can tear it all
down again afterwards.

00:34:40.650 --> 00:34:42.290
So it's a really
good demonstration

00:34:42.290 --> 00:34:44.239
of how we do what
we do and how you

00:34:44.239 --> 00:34:48.247
can use these ephemeral clusters
using a shared Hive metastore.

00:34:51.989 --> 00:34:54.850
So before I came
out here, I said

00:34:54.850 --> 00:34:56.370
I work with a team of about 25.

00:34:56.370 --> 00:34:58.200
So I sent an email
round to everybody

00:34:58.200 --> 00:35:00.200
I worked with that said,
can you just give me

00:35:00.200 --> 00:35:03.360
a grab bag of things I can
talk about at the end, which

00:35:03.360 --> 00:35:05.920
will come under the
banner of lessons learned?

00:35:05.920 --> 00:35:08.695
So here's a few things that
my colleague sent to me.

00:35:08.695 --> 00:35:10.570
I'm just going to go
through them one by one.

00:35:13.710 --> 00:35:17.680
So this shared Hive
metastore is great

00:35:17.680 --> 00:35:20.230
because it allows us to use
these ephemeral clusters.

00:35:20.230 --> 00:35:22.270
But it does make
upgrades very difficult,

00:35:22.270 --> 00:35:26.230
because we've got to coordinate
teams of data scientists,

00:35:26.230 --> 00:35:30.100
teams of data engineers,
and our infrastructure teams

00:35:30.100 --> 00:35:32.350
to make sure that we're not
stepping on anybody's toes

00:35:32.350 --> 00:35:34.060
when we try to
upgrade that stuff.

00:35:34.060 --> 00:35:37.000
We have to upgrade
everything at the same time.

00:35:37.000 --> 00:35:40.400
We're working with Google to
make that a little bit easier.

00:35:40.400 --> 00:35:42.760
We're hoping to anyway.

00:35:42.760 --> 00:35:46.000
But yeah, it is a bit of a pain
point for us at the moment.

00:35:46.000 --> 00:35:47.560
We recommend if
you're going to do

00:35:47.560 --> 00:35:51.400
this you peg Dataproc to a
particular specific minor

00:35:51.400 --> 00:35:52.817
release.

00:35:52.817 --> 00:35:53.900
You don't have to do that.

00:35:53.900 --> 00:35:57.070
You can specify a
particular Dataproc version,

00:35:57.070 --> 00:35:58.720
a major release
version if you want.

00:35:58.720 --> 00:36:01.810
But we have seen
that doing that--

00:36:01.810 --> 00:36:05.950
and then we'll deploy the
latest minor release for you--

00:36:05.950 --> 00:36:09.337
we have seen occasions where
the minor release changes some

00:36:09.337 --> 00:36:11.920
of the scripts that we run, some
of the initialization scripts

00:36:11.920 --> 00:36:12.710
do break.

00:36:12.710 --> 00:36:13.710
So be careful with that.

00:36:13.710 --> 00:36:16.840
Peg it to a minor
release if you can.

00:36:16.840 --> 00:36:22.610
StackDriver logs can become
very chatty, grow very quickly.

00:36:22.610 --> 00:36:24.160
And they can become
very expensive.

00:36:24.160 --> 00:36:26.482
StackDriver's not cheap.

00:36:26.482 --> 00:36:27.940
Certainly not if
you're using a lot

00:36:27.940 --> 00:36:29.750
and you're throwing
lots of logs into it.

00:36:29.750 --> 00:36:32.690
So use log exclusions within
StackDriver if you can.

00:36:32.690 --> 00:36:34.530
And configure your
log4j properties

00:36:34.530 --> 00:36:38.220
so it's not being too chatty.

00:36:38.220 --> 00:36:40.350
I said we're using
Terraform, and we are.

00:36:40.350 --> 00:36:41.850
It's been great for us.

00:36:41.850 --> 00:36:45.210
But be aware that the Terraform
providers or the Google

00:36:45.210 --> 00:36:47.130
specific Terraform
provider generally lags

00:36:47.130 --> 00:36:48.500
what's available in the SDK.

00:36:48.500 --> 00:36:50.250
So you might be using
features in G Cloud,

00:36:50.250 --> 00:36:52.950
for example, that are currently
not supported in Terraform.

00:36:52.950 --> 00:36:55.370
We have experienced
that a few times.

00:36:55.370 --> 00:36:58.920
And we have made upstream
commits to the Terraform Google

00:36:58.920 --> 00:37:00.460
provider.

00:37:00.460 --> 00:37:03.940
But it has been a
bit of a pain for us.

00:37:03.940 --> 00:37:05.740
In shared clusters,
users can easily

00:37:05.740 --> 00:37:09.010
crash the master nodes where
driver runs on the master,

00:37:09.010 --> 00:37:11.360
so change the default
deploy mode to cluster.

00:37:11.360 --> 00:37:13.300
If you're familiar
with using Spark,

00:37:13.300 --> 00:37:15.490
you have two deployment
modes-- client and cluster.

00:37:15.490 --> 00:37:19.900
Where we're using-- where
we have shared clusters,

00:37:19.900 --> 00:37:23.290
it's quite easy for
one data scientist

00:37:23.290 --> 00:37:25.270
to run a nefarious job
which actually brings

00:37:25.270 --> 00:37:26.780
the whole cluster to its knees.

00:37:26.780 --> 00:37:31.300
So for that reason, run with
a deployment mode of cluster,

00:37:31.300 --> 00:37:36.140
and you minimize the
chances of that happening.

00:37:36.140 --> 00:37:37.490
More lessons learned.

00:37:37.490 --> 00:37:39.060
YARN application
masters can fail

00:37:39.060 --> 00:37:41.680
if scheduled on preempted nodes.

00:37:41.680 --> 00:37:43.380
So I explained how
preempted nodes

00:37:43.380 --> 00:37:45.480
are all about how Google
could actually take

00:37:45.480 --> 00:37:46.930
that resource away from us.

00:37:46.930 --> 00:37:50.020
So running masters on
that is not a good idea.

00:37:50.020 --> 00:37:52.860
You can get around this
by using node labeling.

00:37:52.860 --> 00:37:55.110
And I believe there's
also a Dataproc config

00:37:55.110 --> 00:37:58.150
option which has come out which
prevents this from happening.

00:37:58.150 --> 00:37:58.650
So use that.

00:37:58.650 --> 00:38:00.733
And I can see Mikayla in
the back nodding as well.

00:38:00.733 --> 00:38:03.460
So happy to use that.

00:38:03.460 --> 00:38:05.130
The default Spark
config in Dataproc

00:38:05.130 --> 00:38:07.490
can cause an individual
to max out the cluster.

00:38:07.490 --> 00:38:07.990
Yes.

00:38:07.990 --> 00:38:11.170
So we're using these
shared clusters.

00:38:11.170 --> 00:38:16.060
If you don't tune the initial
configuration options,

00:38:16.060 --> 00:38:18.607
we were seeing particular
jobs that our data scientists

00:38:18.607 --> 00:38:20.440
were running were
actually bringing clusters

00:38:20.440 --> 00:38:21.065
to their knees.

00:38:21.065 --> 00:38:24.790
So be careful with the
default memory configuration

00:38:24.790 --> 00:38:27.450
you apply to those jobs.

00:38:27.450 --> 00:38:29.630
Users need the
Spark and YARN UIs,

00:38:29.630 --> 00:38:31.290
but hard to make
secure and available

00:38:31.290 --> 00:38:33.990
via our authentication proxy.

00:38:33.990 --> 00:38:35.760
Anyone that's used
Spark will tell you

00:38:35.760 --> 00:38:37.980
that the YARN and the
Spark UIs are incredibly

00:38:37.980 --> 00:38:40.900
useful for diagnosing
issues within those jobs,

00:38:40.900 --> 00:38:44.562
particularly if you've got
skew in your Spark jobs.

00:38:44.562 --> 00:38:46.020
So we've had a lot
of hassle making

00:38:46.020 --> 00:38:50.190
those UIs available via the
single sign-on authentication

00:38:50.190 --> 00:38:52.130
proxy that we have.

00:38:52.130 --> 00:38:53.460
So something to be aware of.

00:38:53.460 --> 00:38:56.820
Make sure you get those
working as early as possible.

00:38:56.820 --> 00:38:58.560
Conda is a useful
tool for building

00:38:58.560 --> 00:39:01.400
consistent Python environments
across Dataproc and Jupyter

00:39:01.400 --> 00:39:02.190
Notebooks.

00:39:02.190 --> 00:39:06.840
So we need to make sure that the
same Python libraries that we

00:39:06.840 --> 00:39:08.880
deployed to Dataproc
are the same that we

00:39:08.880 --> 00:39:12.720
used in our Jupyter
Notebooks as well.

00:39:12.720 --> 00:39:15.400
Conda is a useful
tool for doing that.

00:39:15.400 --> 00:39:18.930
And finally, GCS retention
policies on temporary data

00:39:18.930 --> 00:39:19.620
sets.

00:39:19.620 --> 00:39:24.810
So we provide specific temporary
areas, so Google storage

00:39:24.810 --> 00:39:27.300
buckets, that our
data scientists

00:39:27.300 --> 00:39:30.430
can use for temporary storage.

00:39:30.430 --> 00:39:32.730
And you can set retention
policies on those

00:39:32.730 --> 00:39:36.290
so that data gets cleared down
after a certain amount of time.

00:39:36.290 --> 00:39:39.450
We actually saw an
occasion last week

00:39:39.450 --> 00:39:43.110
where the usage of one
of our particular clients

00:39:43.110 --> 00:39:46.872
jumped from 17 terabytes
to 36 terabytes overnight.

00:39:46.872 --> 00:39:49.080
And it's basically because
one of our data scientists

00:39:49.080 --> 00:39:51.420
had dropped a load of data into
one of these temporary staging

00:39:51.420 --> 00:39:51.940
areas.

00:39:51.940 --> 00:39:53.430
That happened on a Friday.

00:39:53.430 --> 00:39:55.410
By the end of Monday,
that would automatically

00:39:55.410 --> 00:39:57.858
get cleaned up again because
of the retention policy

00:39:57.858 --> 00:39:59.400
that we put on those
temporary areas.

00:40:03.850 --> 00:40:04.660
Right.

00:40:04.660 --> 00:40:05.480
I am done.

00:40:05.480 --> 00:40:09.040
Thank you very much for
attending, everybody.

00:40:09.040 --> 00:40:12.510
Ubiquitous slide about
providing feedback if you could.

00:40:12.510 --> 00:40:14.560
I'd certainly really
appreciate it.

00:40:14.560 --> 00:40:16.310
I've got about 10 minutes left.

00:40:16.310 --> 00:40:18.810
So if anybody wants to stick
around, I'll take questions.

00:40:18.810 --> 00:40:20.560
And thank you once
again for coming along.

00:40:20.560 --> 00:40:21.850
I hope it was useful.

00:40:21.850 --> 00:40:23.650
